WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.618
[MUSIC]

00:00:09.682 --> 00:00:11.320
What about resumes?

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:15.095
I'm asked a lot of times about resumes,
and

00:00:15.095 --> 00:00:20.414
I probably pay less attention
to resumes than most people do.

00:00:20.414 --> 00:00:21.651
You got to have one?

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:24.282
Maybe.

00:00:24.282 --> 00:00:29.328
I mean, frankly today, your LinkedIn
profile may be good enough for

00:00:29.328 --> 00:00:32.376
most of the things that you've got to do.

00:00:32.376 --> 00:00:36.379
If you don't have a LinkedIn profile,
you got to get one, and do that quick,

00:00:36.379 --> 00:00:40.087
make sure it's always current, and
make sure it's always accurate.

00:00:40.087 --> 00:00:45.200
Beyond a resume, this is a document
that helps you tell your story.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.890
And it will help you, if you have a good
story to tell, and if you tell it right.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:51.980
So what makes a good story?

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:55.268
Well, in my opinion, good choices,

00:00:55.268 --> 00:00:59.880
because you're going to have to be
explaining them with logical explanations.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.967
Because in your career, you're going to be
faced with a number of times, where you're

00:01:03.967 --> 00:01:07.882
going to be sitting down across the table,
from somebody that you want to convince,

00:01:07.882 --> 00:01:10.058
they ought to do something positive for
you.

00:01:10.058 --> 00:01:14.522
And they're probably going to look at your
background, and look at your resume, and

00:01:14.522 --> 00:01:17.757
start asking you questions like,
well, why did you do that?

00:01:17.757 --> 00:01:19.436
Why did you make that choice?

00:01:19.436 --> 00:01:22.560
Why did you go here instead of there?

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.452
Why didn't you stay and
get promoted in this job, if, in fact,

00:01:27.452 --> 00:01:30.906
you were doing as good
as you tell me you were?

00:01:30.906 --> 00:01:34.143
Company and
people over product in compensation,

00:01:34.143 --> 00:01:39.036
I generally advise people to go work for
the best people they can work for, and

00:01:39.036 --> 00:01:42.290
for the best companies they can work for.

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:45.507
If the products not right, and
it's a good company and you're working for

00:01:45.507 --> 00:01:48.240
great people, that'll get fixed over time.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.505
If it's a greatest product in the world
and you're working for lousy people,

00:01:51.505 --> 00:01:54.870
they'll find a way to mess it up,
and you'll be on the outs.

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.550
A venture capitalists
understand this all too well.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:02.219
I mean, one of the oldest sayings in the
venture capital world is an A management

00:02:02.219 --> 00:02:06.140
team with a B product will find
a way to make their investors money.

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:10.390
And a B management team with an A product
will find a way to mess it up.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:13.940
I mean, and that's how they invest.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:16.680
You look at their record, and
you'll see it it's people.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:21.825
Measurable accomplishments and promotions.

00:02:21.825 --> 00:02:26.546
If you can focus on anything when
you're putting your story together,

00:02:26.546 --> 00:02:31.819
focus on what you have accomplished that
you can legitimately take credit for,

00:02:31.819 --> 00:02:34.115
or at least, partial credit for.

00:02:34.115 --> 00:02:37.311
And be clear which is which
when you're explaining it,

00:02:37.311 --> 00:02:42.139
that's measurable with facts and figures
and things that people will recognise and

00:02:42.139 --> 00:02:44.180
ideally, which got you promoted.

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:51.647
Credible transitions and moves as I said,
you're going to be explaining

00:02:51.647 --> 00:02:56.574
your moves over time to people who
are not going to take bullshit or

00:02:56.574 --> 00:03:00.620
fluffy answers and
pay much attention to them.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:04.453
So, think about that before you
make a transition or a move,

00:03:04.453 --> 00:03:09.337
and certainly as you're making and
how you're going to be explaining it 10,

00:03:09.337 --> 00:03:11.980
15, 20 years down the road.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:16.647
And when I scan resumes, I talk to people
about their background, the first thing

00:03:16.647 --> 00:03:21.315
and the last thing and almost everything
in between that I looked at was, where and

00:03:21.315 --> 00:03:23.370
when did this person get promoted?

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:28.870
Because you get promoted when the people
or the company that you work for thought

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:33.381
you did a good enough job and what the job
they gave you, they give you a bigger one.

00:03:33.381 --> 00:03:38.589
And it's amazing when I looked at careers,
would see backgrounds

00:03:38.589 --> 00:03:43.518
of somebody that starts out,
probably not a Stanford MBA, but

00:03:43.518 --> 00:03:47.896
they start out as a district
sales manager of company A.

00:03:47.896 --> 00:03:51.951
And then a regional sales leader
of company B, and then a sales and

00:03:51.951 --> 00:03:56.892
marketing director of company C and a VP
of sales and marketing, a company, and

00:03:56.892 --> 00:03:59.426
a chief operating officer of company F.

00:03:59.426 --> 00:04:01.850
It looks like a great
progression of titles.

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:04.630
They never got promoted, never once.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:09.898
So it makes you wonder where they just get
out of town just before the axe fell on

00:04:09.898 --> 00:04:13.515
them, and getting lucky to use
their background to get promoted to

00:04:13.515 --> 00:04:17.344
another company that would buy their
story and give them a bigger job.

00:04:17.344 --> 00:04:21.526
When you look around, you'll see people
like this all the time, and they're not

00:04:21.526 --> 00:04:25.603
the people you want to go work for it for
one thing, they're going to be splitting.

00:04:25.603 --> 00:04:27.234
They are not going to take care
of you because they're only

00:04:27.234 --> 00:04:28.690
concerned about themselves.

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:30.583
And you don't want to
be one of those people.

00:04:30.583 --> 00:04:34.972
So think about if you're close,
if you've got an opportunity, or

00:04:34.972 --> 00:04:39.900
you're thinking about leaving and
you're close to finishing a project you

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:44.905
can take credit for, and getting a
promotion, I would urge you think long and

00:04:44.905 --> 00:04:48.694
hard before you leave anywhere
just short of a promotion.

00:04:48.694 --> 00:04:50.955
Because somebody like
me is going to ask you,

00:04:50.955 --> 00:04:54.894
why did you leave just shorter when you
should have probably gotten promoted?

00:04:54.894 --> 00:04:57.486
Where you're going to get promoted?

00:04:57.486 --> 00:05:02.084
And finally, what makes a good story
when you're telling it to somebody is at

00:05:02.084 --> 00:05:06.537
the end of the day, at the end of
the conversation, you've got to be able to

00:05:06.537 --> 00:05:10.781
convince them that you can be successful
doing something that in fact,

00:05:10.781 --> 00:05:11.800
they want done.

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:15.123
Because if you can convince
them that you can do it, but

00:05:15.123 --> 00:05:18.980
it isn't anything that they need to
have done, then you won't go anywhere.

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:21.213
Or if they have something that
they need to have done and

00:05:21.213 --> 00:05:23.500
you can't convince them
that you can do it.

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:24.720
It doesn't go anywhere.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.304
It's only when you put
those two things together,

00:05:27.304 --> 00:05:29.948
that telling your story to
somebody that matters and

00:05:29.948 --> 00:05:33.210
can do something positive with it,
will get you a good outcome.

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:37.185
How do you tell it?
Well, we've touched on some of this.

00:05:37.185 --> 00:05:40.190
Clarity of goals and accomplishments.

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:44.473
I'm basically an advocate of a simple
chronological resume that says,

00:05:44.473 --> 00:05:48.819
what you did, when you did it,
who you did it for, what the results were,

00:05:48.819 --> 00:05:51.850
and what happened to you as a result.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:56.050
I mean, if you think about those things,
that's what I'm always looking for.

00:05:56.050 --> 00:06:01.420
I don't like a lot of fluffy explanations
of things that don't matter.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:04.090
Facts are good if they're true.

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:05.780
Promotions are better.

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:10.630
Accurate facts and dates,
easy to pick out successes.

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:14.444
Keep in mind that you want somebody
who's looking at your resume, or

00:06:14.444 --> 00:06:18.062
looking at your LinkedIn profile
to look at something on there and

00:06:18.062 --> 00:06:20.196
say, I can use that, that fits here.

00:06:20.196 --> 00:06:22.122
That's interesting.

00:06:22.122 --> 00:06:25.890
Logical transitions,
no misrepresentations or

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:30.474
vague claims, and
a clear incredible path to what you seek.

00:06:30.474 --> 00:06:35.017
[MUSIC]

